WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:03.420
Hello and welcome back.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:06.760
Last week you learned about RNNs, GRUs and LSTMs.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:13.060
In this week, you see how many of these ideas can be applied to NLP, to Natural Language Processing,

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:17.620
which is one of the fields of AI that is really being revolutionized by deep learning.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.140
One of the key ideas you learn about is word embeddings, which is a way of representing words

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.220
that lets your algorithms automatically understand analogies like that

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.320
Manister Woman as King of Siqueen and many other examples.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.360
And through these ideas of word embeddings, you'll be able to build NLP applications even

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.400
with modestly sized or even with relatively small labeled training sets.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.240
Finally, towards the end of the week, you see how to debias word embeddings as to reduce

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.520
undesirable gender or ethnicity or other types of bias that learning algorithms can

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.040
sometimes pick up.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.840
So with that, let's get started with a discussion on word representation.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.240
So far, we've been representing words using a vocabulary of words.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:09.200
And vocabulary from the previous week might be say 10,000 words.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.560
And we've been representing words using a one-hot vector.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:19.800
So for example, if man is word number 5391 in this dictionary, then you represent it

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.360
with a vector with a 1 in position 5391.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:32.720
And I'm also going to use O subscript 5391 to represent this vector where O here stands

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.000
for one-hot.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:44.760
And then if woman is word number 9853, then you represent it with O subscript 9853, which

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:49.480
just says a 1 in position 9853 and 0's elsewhere.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:56.040
And then other words, king, queen, apple, orange would be similarly represented with

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.160
one-hot vectors.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.800
One of the weaknesses of this representation is that it treats each word as a thing unto

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:10.440
itself and it doesn't allow an algorithm to easily generalize across words.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.800
For example, let's say you have a language model that has learned that when you see

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.120
I want a glass of orange blank, well what do you think the next word will be?

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:22.840
Very likely to be juice.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:28.280
But even if the learning algorithm has learned that I want a glass of orange juice is a likely

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.320
sentence, if it sees I want a glass of apple blank, as far as it knows, the relationship

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:40.160
between apple and orange is not any closer as the relationship between any of the other

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.560
words, man, woman, king, queen and orange.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.640
And so it's not easy for the learning algorithm to generalize from knowing that orange juice

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:54.320
is a popular thing to recognizing that apple juice might also be a popular thing or a popular

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.000
phrase.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.920
And this is because the inner product between any two different one-hot vectors is 0.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.160
If you take any two vectors, say queen and king and inner product them, the inner product

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.160
is 0.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.600
If you take apple and orange and inner product them, the inner product is 0.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:17.120
And Euclidean distance between any pair of these vectors is also the same.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.780
So it just doesn't know that somehow apple and orange are much more similar than king

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.820
and orange or queen and orange.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:29.560
So want to be nice if instead of a one-hot representation, we can instead learn a featurized

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:34.040
representation where for each of these words, man, woman, king, queen, apple, orange,

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:39.160
or really for every word in our dictionary, we could learn a set of features and values

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.120
for each of them.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.160
So for example, each of these words might have, we want to know what is the gender associated

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:47.160
with each of these things.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:53.280
So if gender goes from minus 1 for male to plus 1 for female, then the gender associated

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.400
man might be minus 1 for woman might be plus 1.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.640
And then if you're actually learning these things, maybe for king you get minus 0.95

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:07.840
for queen plus 0.97 and for apple and orange, you know, sort of genderless.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.240
Another feature might be, well, how royal are these things?

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:16.280
And so the terms man and woman are not particularly royal.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:22.320
So the features values close to 0, whereas king and queen are highly royal and apple

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.800
and orange are not particularly royal.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:26.800
How about age?

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.840
Well, man and woman doesn't connote much about age.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:36.680
Maybe man and woman implies that they're adults, but maybe neither necessarily young

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:44.640
nor old, so maybe values close to 0, whereas kings and queens are almost always adults and

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.320
apple and orange might be more neutral for respect to age.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.680
And then another feature for is this a food?

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.000
Well, man is not a food, woman is not a food.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.240
There are kings and queens, but apples and oranges are foods.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:07.120
And there can be many other features as well, ranging from what is the size of this, what

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:13.560
is the cost, is this something that is alive, is this an action, or is this a noun, or is

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:17.600
this a verb, or is this something else, and so on.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.400
So you can imagine coming up with many features, and for the sake of illustration, let's say

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:26.160
300 different features.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.800
And what that does is it allows you to take this list of numbers, I've only written four

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:37.520
here, but this could be a list of maybe 300 numbers, that then becomes a 300-dimensional

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.680
vector for representing the word man.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:51.120
And I'm going to use the notation E subscript 5, 3, 9, 1 to denote a representation like

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.400
this.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:58.160
And similarly, this vector, this 300-dimensional vector, or 300-dimensional vector like this,

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:07.320
I would denote E 9, 8, 5, 3 to denote a 300-dimensional vector we could use to represent the word woman.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:11.120
And similarly, for the other examples here.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:18.640
Now if you use this representation to represent the words orange and apple, then notice that

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.560
the representations for orange and apple are now quite similar.

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:28.160
Some of the features will differ, because maybe the color of an orange and apple, the

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.160
taste, or some of the features will differ.

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:36.960
But by and large, a lot of features of apple and orange are actually the same, or take

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:38.680
on very similar values.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:43.160
And so this increases the odds of the learning algorithm that has figured out that orange

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:48.040
juice is a thing, to also quickly figure out that apple juice is a thing.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:52.360
So this allows it to generalize better across different words.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:56.720
So over the next few videos, we'll find a way to learn word embeddings, which is basically

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:02.000
to learn high-dimensional feature vectors like these that gives a better representation than

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.120
one-hot vectors for representing different words.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:12.240
And the features we'll end up learning won't have an easy to interpret interpretation like

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:17.800
that component 1 is gender, component 2 is royal, component 3 is Asian, and so on.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:22.360
Maybe what they're representing will be a bit harder to figure out, but nonetheless, the

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:27.080
featurized representations we'll be learning will allow an algorithm to quickly figure out

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:34.240
that apple and orange are more similar than, say, king and orange or queen and orange.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:41.520
If we're able to learn a 300-dimensional feature vector or 300-dimensional embedding,

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.000
for each word, one of the popular things to do is also to take this 300-dimensional

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:53.160
data and embed it, say, in a two-dimensional space so that you can visualize them.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:08:00.120
And so one common algorithm for doing this is the T-SNE algorithm due to Lauren Van der

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:02.360
Martin and Jeff Hinton.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:08.040
And if you look at one of these embeddings, one of these representations, you find that

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:12.400
words like man and woman tend to get grouped together, king and queen tend to get grouped

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:17.280
together, and these are the people which tend to get grouped together.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:20.320
Those are animals who tend to get grouped together.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:22.800
The fruits will tend to be close to each other.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.600
Numbers like 1, 2, 3, 4 will be close to each other.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:31.360
And then maybe the animated objects as a whole will also tend to get grouped together.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:37.360
But you see plots like these sometimes on the internet to visualize some of these 300- or

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:41.040
higher-dimensional embeddings.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:48.200
Maybe this gives you a sense that word embedding algorithms like these can learn similar features

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:54.480
for concepts that feel like they should be more related, as visualized by concepts that

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:59.680
seem to you and me like they should be more similar, end up getting mapped to more similar

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.160
feature vectors.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:07.200
And these representations will use these sort of feature-wise representations in maybe a

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:08.960
300-dimensional space.

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:10.920
These are called embeddings.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:16.560
And the reason we call them embeddings is you can think of a 300-dimensional space, and

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.560
again, I can't draw a 300-dimensional space.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:20.360
This is maybe a 3D one.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:25.840
And what you do is you take every word like orange and you have a 300-dimensional feature

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:26.840
vector.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:32.120
So the word orange gets embedded to a point in this 300-dimensional space.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:38.240
And the word apple gets embedded to a different point in this 300-dimensional space.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:42.600
And of course, to visualize it, algorithms like T-SNE map this to a much lower-dimensional

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:48.200
space so you can actually plot the 2D data and look at it.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:50.960
But that's where the term embedding comes from.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:55.320
Word embeddings has been one of the most important ideas in NLP, in natural language

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:56.320
processing.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:01.320
In this video, you saw why you might want to learn or use word embeddings.

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:04.720
In the next video, let's take a deeper look at how you'll be able to use these

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:07.160
algorithms to build NLP algorithms.
